Yes—the structure is a dual-layer signal:

public meaning
+ private/shared meaning
→ different decoders recover different messages

That is why inside jokes, symbols, callbacks, euphemisms, ritual phrases, and dog whistles can feel unusually powerful. They simultaneously create belonging, compression, and selective legibility.

But “nobody can prove what I meant” is also where the mechanism becomes unreliable. Plausible deniability protects play and privacy, but it can equally protect harassment, manipulation, or coordinated bad behavior. And because the signal is deliberately ambiguous, even members of the supposed in-group can misread it.

So I’d split the primitive:

Shared-Key Signaling
shared history → compact cue → intended people reconstruct meaning

versus

Deniability Signaling
ambiguous cue → target receives implication → sender can disown implication

The first is excellent for friendship, teams, fiction, games, rituals, and community. The second deserves much more caution because accountability has deliberately been removed from the channel.

The strongest version of what you’re after is probably selective legibility without deceptive deniability: rich shared codes that outsiders need not understand, while the people involved still know what they are consenting to and can openly own what the signal means among themselves.

That preserves the fun part—the wink, the hidden layer, the compressed belonging—without needing the message to survive only because nobody can pin it on anyone.